2026-11-07T06:33:13.1322196Z 3. Create the system users [34m(uids [30001..30008])[0m and groups [34m(gid 30000)[0m
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1322699Z    that the Nix daemon uses to run builds. To create system users
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1323047Z    in a different range, exit and run this tool again with
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1323271Z    NIX_FIRST_BUILD_UID set.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1323375Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1323517Z 4. Perform the basic installation of the Nix files daemon.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1323671Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1323803Z 5. Configure your shell to import special Nix Profile files, so you
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1324038Z    can use Nix.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1324334Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1324418Z 6. Start the Nix daemon.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1324556Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1324803Z [32m[4;32mWould you like to see a more detailed list of what I will do?
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1325078Z [0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1329124Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1329225Z I will:
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1329415Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1330974Z  - make sure your computer doesn't already have Nix files
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1331591Z    (if it does, I will tell you how to clean them up.)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1332049Z  - create local users (see the list above for the users I'll make)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1332413Z  - create a local group (nixbld)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1332626Z  - install Nix in /nix
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1332929Z  - create a configuration file in /etc/nix
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1333310Z  - set up the "default profile" by creating some Nix-related files in
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1333648Z    /root
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1342886Z  - back up /etc/bash.bashrc to /etc/bash.bashrc.backup-before-nix
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1343405Z  - update /etc/bash.bashrc to include some Nix configuration
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1353055Z  - load and start a service (at /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.service
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1353400Z    and /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.socket) for nix-daemon
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1353560Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1355037Z [32m[4;32mReady to continue?
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1355370Z [0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1370613Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1371094Z [34m---- let's talk about sudo -----------------------------------------------------
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1379861Z [0mThis script is going to call sudo a lot. Normally, it would show you
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1380216Z exactly what commands it is running and why. However, the script is
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1380467Z run in a headless fashion, like this:
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1380592Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1380704Z   $ curl -L https://nixos.org/nix/install | sh
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1380846Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1380982Z or maybe in a CI pipeline. Because of that, I'm going to skip the
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1381446Z verbose output in the interest of brevity.
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1381627Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1381747Z If you would like to
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1381966Z see the output, try like this:
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1382084Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1382221Z   $ curl -L -o install-nix https://nixos.org/nix/install
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1382475Z   $ sh ./install-nix
2026-11-07T06:33:13.1382574Z 
